<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mads Lange</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1807">1807</a>–<a href="/years/1856">1856</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mads Johansen Lange</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mads Lange was a Danish trader who ran a large trading post at Kuta in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> from the late 1830s under the protection of the raja of Badung. He exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, coconut oil and other goods, supplied passing ships and acted as a go-between in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> campaigns against Bali's kingdoms in the 1840s. His business declined in the 1850s, and he died at Kuta in 1856.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mads-lange.jpg" alt="Mads Lange" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chinese painter, name not mentioned in book · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mads_Johansen_Lange.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mads Johansen Lange was born in Rudkøbing on 18 September 1807, the second son of the merchant Lorents Lange Pedersen and Maren Hansen, who ran a tavern and a shop she had inherited. He went to sea at fourteen and from 1824 served on the ship North under Captain John Burd, sailing with him for many years, including to the Danish trading post of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tharangambadi">Tranquebar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. After a stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1833 and 1834 he and Burd bought the ship South and sailed for India and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, with Burd as captain, Lange as first mate and three of Lange's younger brothers aboard. None of the four brothers saw Denmark again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1835 he settled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a>, where the Raja of Karangasem Lombok let him trade from Tanjungkarang for a yearly fee; he exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and tobacco and imported textiles and weapons. When war between the island's states culminated in his patron's defeat in 1839, he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> in his schooner Venus. Invited by Raja Kassiman of Badung, he built his factorij near Kotta, now Kuta, on a narrow peninsula where ships could load on either side, profiting from a rice shortage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and Bali's three harvests a year.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> sent the First Dutch Expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> in 1846 and took Buleleng and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singaraja">Singaraja</a>, Lange negotiated with the Rajas of Buleleng and Karangasem a peace under which the Rajas paid small damages and the Dutch kept a small force on the island. The war, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockades</a> and the rise of steamers, which drew trade to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, left his business in decline, and his oil mill stopped for want of coconuts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Lange sent artefacts to the Royal Ethnographic Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, for which Frederik VII awarded him the Danish Golden Medal of Merit on 28 July 1854, and money to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> during the First Danish-German War. He died on 13 May 1856; his daughter Cecilia and his English physician suspected poison, which was never proved. His brother Hans and nephew Peter Christian kept the business going until Raja Kassiman's death in 1863, after which it was sold to a Chinese merchant. With the Balinese princess Nyai Kenyèr and a Chinese woman he had three children; Cecilia's son Ibrahim became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> government raised an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisk</a> over his grave in 1927.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mads Johansen Lange” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the mid 19th century the Danish trader <b>Mads Lange</b> ran a large trading post at Kuta under royal protection.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-badung">Kingdom of Badung</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rudkøbing, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. Died in Kuta, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.94,10.72&amp;year=1838" aria-label="Rudkøbing on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mads Lange"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5523 5390h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5523 5390h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.94,10.72&amp;year=1838">What was here in 1838? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
